===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Accordance with [[moral]] principles; conformity of [[behaviour]] or thought with the strictures of [[morality]]; good moral conduct. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Without '''virtue''', there is no freedom.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|passage=||XV|1|text=There are a set of religious, or rather moral, writers, who teach that '''virtue''' is the certain road to happiness, and vice to misery, in this world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=Oct 1 2004|publisher=w:Wizards of the Coast|title=Champions of Kamigawa|chapter=Eight-and-a-Half-Tails|text=“'''Virtue''' is an inner light that can prevail in every soul.”}}
# A particular [[manifestation]] of moral [[excellence]] in a person; an admirable [[quality]]. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1766|author=Laurence Sterne|title=Sermon|section=XLIV
|passage=Some men are modest, and seem to take pains to hide their '''virtues'''; and, from a natural distance and reserve in their tempers, scarce suffer their good qualities to be known {{...}}.}}
# Specifically, each of several qualities held to be particularly important, including the four [[cardinal virtues]], the three [[theological virtues]], or the seven virtues opposed to the seven deadly sins. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1813|author=John Fleetwood|title=The Life of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ|passage=The divine '''virtues''' of truth and equity are the only bands of friendship, the only supports of society.}}
# An inherently advantageous or excellent [[quality]] of something or someone; a favourable point, an [[advantage]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Robinson Crusoe|passage=There were divers other plants, which I had no notion of or understanding about, that might, perhaps, have '''virtues''' of their own, which I could not find out.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2011|journal=The Guardian|section=Letter, 14 Mar 2011
|passage=One '''virtue''' of the present coalition government's attack on access to education could be to reopen the questions raised so pertinently by Robinson in the 1960s {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} A creature embodying divine power, specifically one of the [[order]]s of heavenly beings, traditionally ranked above angels and [[archangel]]s, and below [[seraph|seraphim]] and [[cherub|cherubim]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=X|text=Thrones, Dominations, Princedoms, '''Virtues''', Powers; / For in possession such, not only of right, / I call ye, and declare ye now {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Specifically, moral conduct in [[sexual]] behaviour, especially of women; [[chastity]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1813|author=Jane Austen|title=Pride and Prejudice|passage=though she did not suppose Lydia to be deliberately engaging in an elopement without the intention of marriage, she had no difficulty in believing that neither her '''virtue''' nor her understanding would preserve her from falling an easy prey.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The inherent [[power]] of a [[god]], or other supernatural being. {{defdate|13th–19th c.}}
# The inherent [[power]] or [[efficacy]] of something {{qualifier|now only in phrases}}. {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Southey Thalaba|||There was a '''virtue''' in the wave;<br>His limbs, that, stiff with toil,<br>Dragg’d heavy, from the copious draught receiv’d<br>Lightness and supple strength.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Madeleine L'Engle|Madeleine L’Engle]]|chapter=Aunt Beast|title=w:A Wrinkle in Time|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Farrar, Straus and Giroux|Ariel Books]]|year=1962|oclc=769806129|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=Ariel Books|year2=1973 printing|page2=189|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/wrinkleintime00lengrich/page/189/mode/1up|isbn2=978-0-374-38613-9|passage=Here are the glasses, Meg. But I am afraid that the '''virtue''' has gone from them, and now they are only glass. Perhaps they were meant to help once and only on Camazotz.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The autumn of the patriarchs|journal=The Economist|date=17 Feb 2011
|passage=many Egyptians still worry that the Brotherhood, by ''virtue'' of discipline and experience, would hold an unfair advantage if elections were held too soon.}}

